<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thesmophoria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thesmophoria was an ancient Greek religious festival in honour of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> and her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persephone">Persephone</a>, held each year, mostly around the sowing of seed in late autumn, to promote human and agricultural fertility. Among the most widely celebrated Greek festivals, it was restricted to adult women, and its rites were kept secret. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> it lasted three days, the 11th to the 13th of Pyanepsion; the second, the nesteia, was a day of fasting imitating Demeter's mourning. Pigs were sacrificed and put into pits called megara, and their rotten remains were later retrieved and scattered on the fields with the seed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes">Aristophanes</a> parodied the festival in his Thesmophoriazusae.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The priestess of Athena Polias was one of the most prominent figures in Athens, the Pythia at Delphi spoke for Apollo, and festivals such as the <b>Thesmophoria</b> of Demeter were reserved for women.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-greek-religion">Ancient Greek religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion gave women their largest public role: the priestess of Athena Polias had real influence, and citizen women ran the <b>Thesmophoria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
